                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want one rule: polyurethane when the wall is dry, hybrid polymer when it is not, self-leveling polyurethane on the floor, and neither silicone nor acrylic latex anywhere you actually expect water. Every one of these is sold in a standard ten-ounce cartridge that fits an ordinary caulk gun, so the choice is purely about chemistry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Caulk a Basement Wall Crack, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sealant is the easy part. Almost every caulked crack that fails does so because of preparation, specifically dust, efflorescence or moisture sitting between the bead and the concrete. Give the prep twice as long as the application and the bead will outlast your interest in checking it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Clean the Crack Back to Sound Concrete</h3>

          <p>
            Scrub the crack and a strip about an inch either side with a stiff wire brush, then vacuum it out with a shop vac and a crevice nozzle. Remove any old caulk completely, and if it was silicone, wire-wheel the surface until no shiny film remains. The white, powdery crust called efflorescence is mineral salt carried out by water, and it must go: sealant bonded to efflorescence is bonded to a layer of powder. Brush it off dry, and if you wash the area, give it days to dry out afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Decide Whether to V-Groove the Crack</h3>

          <p>
            A bead run over a true hairline crack has almost nothing to grip except the surface either side, and it ends up as a thin film that tears easily. Widening the mouth of the crack into a shallow V about a quarter of an inch wide and a quarter of an inch deep, using an angle grinder with a diamond blade or a crack-chasing wheel, gives the sealant a proper channel to fill. It is loud and dusty, and concrete dust contains crystalline silica, so wear a properly fitted respirator and eye protection and run a vacuum at the blade. For a crack you are sealing only against dust or soil gas, careful surface prep without grooving is an acceptable shortcut. Do not undercut the groove the way you would for hydraulic cement; a simple V is what sealant wants.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Use Backer Rod on Anything Over a Quarter Inch</h3>

          <p>
            For a crack or groove wider than about a quarter of an inch, press closed-cell foam backer rod into it first, choosing a rod roughly a quarter larger than the gap so it grips. Set it so the sealant depth ends up about half the width, never less than a quarter of an inch. The rod stops the sealant bonding to the back of the joint. A bead stuck on three sides cannot stretch when the crack moves and tears down the middle, while a bead bonded only to the two sides flexes like a rubber band. It also saves you from pumping a whole tube into a deep crack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Make Sure the Surface Is Actually Dry</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete that looks dry often is not. Tape a square of clear plastic sheet, about eighteen inches across, tightly over the area and leave it for a day. If the concrete underneath has darkened or droplets have formed under the plastic, moisture is coming through the wall. Either wait for a drier spell, run a dehumidifier for a few days, or switch to a hybrid polymer that tolerates damp. Most sealants also want the air and surface above about forty degrees Fahrenheit, which an unheated basement in winter may not reach. Our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> covers getting a damp space dry and keeping it that way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Apply and Tool the Bead</h3>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle small, at an angle, so the bead is slightly wider than the crack. Push the gun along the crack rather than dragging it, which forces sealant into the gap instead of laying it on top. Polyurethane is thick, especially in a cold basement, so a gun with a decent thrust ratio makes the job far less tiring; our guide to the <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">best caulk gun</a> explains what to look for. Leaving the tube in a warm room for a few hours beforehand helps too. Tool the bead within a few minutes with a caulk tool or a gloved finger dipped in soapy water, pressing it into the crack and feathering the edges onto clean concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">6. Let It Cure Before Painting or Finishing</h3>

          <p>
            Polyurethane skins over in hours but cures from the outside in, and a thick bead in a cool basement can take several days to a week or more to cure through. Hybrids are generally quicker. Keep the area ventilated, keep it dry, and do not paint, coat or frame over it until the tube's stated cure time has passed. If a masonry waterproofing coating is next, our roundup of the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealers</a> covers which ones play well over cured sealant.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Caulk vs a DIY Injection Kit: The Honest Cost Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            On price alone, caulk wins by a mile. A cartridge of polyurethane or hybrid concrete sealant typically costs well under fifteen dollars, and a ten-ounce tube holds a little over eighteen cubic inches of material. Run as a quarter-inch by quarter-inch bead, that works out to roughly twenty-four linear feet, which covers two or three typical wall cracks. Add a few dollars for backer rod if you need it and a caulk gun if you do not own one, and a whole basement's worth of hairline cracks is usually sealed for less than thirty dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            A DIY polyurethane or epoxy injection kit runs roughly fifty to a hundred and twenty dollars and typically handles one crack of up to about ten feet. A professional injection visit is commonly quoted in the four to eight hundred dollar range per crack, often with a warranty attached. Those are the figures we use in our full crack repair guide, and they have not changed the basic picture: injection costs four to ten times as much per crack as caulk.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Cost Per Crack</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$10-30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Caulk and backer rod</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$50-120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DIY injection kit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$400-800</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Professional injection</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~24 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Bead per 10-oz tube</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The price comparison is only honest if you include the cost of guessing wrong. Caulk on a crack that turns out to leak under pressure does not just fail; it has to be cut and ground off before an injection kit's surface paste will bond, because the ports and paste need clean concrete. So the realistic cost of caulking the wrong crack is the tube, the afternoon, the removal, and then the injection kit anyway, plus whatever the water did in between. Cheap is only cheap when it works first time.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gives a simple rule. If a crack has ever carried running water, the injection kit is the cheapest option, because it is the only one of the two that will work on the first attempt. If a crack has only ever been dry or showed a damp line, caulk is the smart money, and paying for injection is spending five times as much to solve a problem you do not have.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more comparison worth making: spray-on rubberised coatings. They are sometimes marketed as the one-can fix for a leaking basement wall, and on a crack they behave much like caulk, bonding to the face and losing to pressure from behind. Our test of <a href="/flex-seal-for-basement-walls/">Flex Seal for basement walls</a> goes into why. For a dry hairline crack, a properly tooled bead of polyurethane is a better and cheaper repair than a sprayed patch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Caulk Cannot Do, and What to Do Instead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caulk cannot stop an active leak under pressure. When the soil outside is saturated, water collects against the wall and pushes through any path it can find, and a crack is the easiest path there is. The water fills the crack from the back and presses on the rear of a bead that was only ever bonded at the edges. It will blister, lift and then leak, usually during exactly the storm you bought it for. The right sequence is a <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/">hydraulic cement</a> plug if it is running now, then polyurethane injection, then a look at why the soil is that wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caulk cannot fix a wide or displaced crack. A crack wider than about a quarter of an inch, or one where one face sits even slightly proud of the other, means the wall has moved more than shrinkage explains. Backer rod and sealant will close the gap and make the wall look tidy, and that is the problem: it hides the evidence you need to watch. Mark it, measure it and have it looked at before you seal anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caulk has no business on a structural crack at all. Horizontal cracks partway up a wall, stair-step cracks through a block wall that show displacement, and any wall that bows inward when you hold a long level against it are signs of soil pressure acting on the foundation. The fix for those is structural, from reinforcement straps to bracing to anchors, and it comes from a structural engineer rather than a tube. A tidy line of sealant over a bowing wall is not a repair, and a home inspector who sees one will ask what it was covering.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry, stable and under a quarter inch: caulk it. Anything else: find out why first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Running water goes to hydraulic cement and injection. Wide or offset cracks go to measurement and monitoring. Horizontal, stair-step or bowing walls go to a structural engineer. Caulk is a finishing move for the easy cases, not a diagnosis-free shortcut for the hard ones.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Caulk also will not help a wall that is sweating rather than leaking. Warm, humid summer air meeting cool concrete condenses on the face of the wall and can look exactly like seepage. The plastic sheet test from the step-by-step section tells you which one you have: moisture on top of the plastic is condensation from the room, moisture underneath is coming through the wall. Condensation is a humidity problem, and sealing cracks does nothing for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            And caulk will not fix a basement where water arrives regardless. If several cracks leak, if the cove joint runs after every storm, or if you are sealing new damp spots every spring, the wall is telling you the water management outside or under the slab is not coping. That is a job for gutters and grading first, and then for an interior or exterior drainage system, which our guide to <a href="/basement-drainage-system/">basement drainage systems</a> explains. For the full comparison of fixes, start with our <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/">basement waterproofing systems</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for Bathroom Silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acetoxy silicone bonds poorly to porous, alkaline concrete and usually peels away within a season. Worse, it leaves a film in the pores that stops polyurethane, paint or injection paste bonding afterward, so the real repair starts with grinding the residue off. Buy a polyurethane or hybrid concrete sealant instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking Over Efflorescence or Damp Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The white crust on a basement crack is mineral salt, and sealant laid over it is bonded to powder. Polyurethane on damp concrete fails the same way. Wire-brush the salts off, vacuum the crack, run the plastic sheet test, and switch to a hybrid polymer if the wall will not dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking a Crack That Runs Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A crack that drips or runs during storms has water pushing from behind, and a surface bead cannot resist that pressure for long. It blisters, lifts and leaks, then has to be removed before injection. If a crack has ever carried running water, skip the caulk and inject it properly the first time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Backer Rod on Wider Cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filling a deep crack with sealant bonds the bead to three faces, so it cannot stretch when the crack moves and tears down the middle. It also wastes most of a tube. For anything over about a quarter inch, press in closed-cell backer rod first and keep sealant depth near half its width.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Structural Crack to Tidy It Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A horizontal crack, a stair-step crack with displacement, or a bowing wall is evidence of soil pressure, and caulking it removes the evidence without touching the cause. It also looks like concealment to any inspector. Measure and monitor it, and get a structural engineer's opinion before sealing anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk basement wall cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the crack is dry or only damp, stable and no wider than about a quarter inch. Hairline shrinkage cracks in poured concrete walls are ideal candidates. Use a polyurethane or hybrid polymer concrete sealant, clean the crack thoroughly, and tool the bead into it. Cracks that run water, have widened or show one side offset need injection or professional assessment instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does silicone sealant work on basement wall cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Common acetoxy silicone, the vinegar-smelling kind, bonds poorly to porous, alkaline concrete and tends to peel off within a season. It cannot be painted, and its residue blocks later repairs. Neutral-cure masonry silicones bond better, but polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealants do the job more reliably indoors and can be painted once they have cured.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for basement wall cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a dry wall, a gun-grade polyurethane concrete sealant, ideally one rated to ASTM C920. For a wall that stays damp, a hybrid or MS polymer sealant formulated for damp surfaces. For floor cracks and the floor side of the cove joint, self-leveling polyurethane. Avoid acetoxy silicone and treat acrylic latex as a cosmetic filler rather than a water barrier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk a basement crack that is damp?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with the right product. Polyurethane wants a dry surface and will not bond reliably to damp concrete. Many hybrid polymer sealants are formulated to tolerate damp surfaces, so they are the better choice for a wall with a persistent damp line. Neither works on a crack that is actively dripping; that needs hydraulic cement to stop it and injection to fix it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide a crack can you caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hairline cracks up to about a quarter inch are practical for caulk. Above roughly a quarter inch, press closed-cell backer rod in first so the sealant depth stays around half its width. But a crack that wide in a basement wall usually means more movement than shrinkage explains, so measure it, check whether it is growing, and consider an assessment before sealing it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is caulk or injection better for sealing basement cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They solve different problems. Caulk seals the face of a dry, stable crack for a few dollars. Injection fills the full depth of the crack and resists water pushing from outside, so it is the right repair for any crack that has leaked. A DIY injection kit costs roughly fifty to a hundred and twenty dollars, several times the price of a tube, but works first time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does caulk last on a basement wall crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-bonded polyurethane or hybrid bead indoors, out of sunlight and on a stable crack, commonly lasts many years. Failures almost always come early and show as edges lifting, usually because of dust, efflorescence or moisture during application, or because the crack was still moving. Check caulked cracks each spring, and treat a bead that lifts as a sign the crack needs more.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

