        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Caulk is a good repair for a narrow job. On a dry or damp hairline crack that is not moving, a properly prepared, properly tooled bead of polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealant costs a few dollars and lasts for years. Skip the silicone, clean off the efflorescence, get the wall dry, and use backer rod on anything wide.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Everything outside that job belongs to something else. Cracks that run water need injection, wide or offset cracks need measuring before sealing, and horizontal or bowing walls need an engineer. If you find yourself caulking new cracks every spring, the problem is the water outside the wall, and that is where the money should go.
          </p>
        </div>

